course: dictionary
lesson: 1192
title: Brushwood
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Brushwood is a noun for small broken branches and twigs, the kind that lie on the ground or are cut from trees. It is also the name for thick, low undergrowth, which is a tangle of small bushes and shrubs. Brushwood is uncountable, so we say some brushwood, not a brushwood.
explain: In the sentence «creepers, brushwood, and dead leaves», the words in the list are all things from plants. The Mole is hiding a tunnel entrance with them, so brushwood must be plant material lying around, not a tool or a stone. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Plant bits would.
explain: Think of a forest where the Mole stumbles through the brushwood of the Wild Wood. The ground is covered in tangled twigs and small bushes, which makes it hard to walk through. That is the second meaning of brushwood in action.
fact: Brushwood is an uncountable noun, so we say some brushwood, not a brushwood.
fact: «Brushwood» means small broken branches and twigs, or thick, low undergrowth of small bushes and shrubs.
fact: Dry brushwood catches fire quickly, so it must be handled with care near a campfire.
fact: People gather brushwood to start a campfire.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, the Mole stumbles through the brushwood of the Wild Wood.
exercise: What is brushwood? | A big shiny leaf from a palm tree | A tall tower of grey stone | *Small broken branches and twigs | A kind of river fish
exercise: Find and copy the word from «creepers, brushwood, and dead leaves» that means small broken branches and twigs. | creepers | and | dead | *brushwood
exercise: Why would a camper gather brushwood? | *To start a campfire | To make a dangerous place to walk | To grow a tall tree | To paint a picture of the sea
test: Mia says dry brushwood is safe to pile right next to a fire. Is she right? | *No, dry brushwood catches fire quickly | Only if it is green | Yes, because it is cold and wet | Yes, because it never burns
test: Which meaning fits a thick, low tangle of small bushes? | *Thick, low undergrowth of small bushes and shrubs | A smooth stone path | A sandy beach by the sea | A tall, straight tree trunk
exam: Which sentence uses brushwood to mean a tangle of small bushes? | The Mole counted his coins | The Mole ate a plate of hot soup | The Mole stopped to read a map | *The Mole stumbled through the brushwood of the Wild Wood
